   },
   "source": [
    "### Results (1 pt)\n",
    "Choose some metric for determining how good the compression with SVD is. Experiment with other compression algorithms (DCT, for instance). Explain the chosen method(-s) for compession. Compare it with SVD. Be creative;)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 103,
   "metadata": {
    "id": "5PVMZww_V1C0"
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def reconstruct_image(image, k):\n",
    "    approx = np.zeros_like(image, dtype=np.float32)\n",
    "    for c in range(3):\n",
    "        U, s, Vt = np.linalg.svd(image[:, :, c], full_matrices=False)\n",
    "        approx[:, :, c] = (U[:, :k] * s[:k]) @ Vt[:k, :]\n",
    "    return np.clip(approx, 0, 255)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Chosen (3) metrics for evaluating comression quality\n",
    " \n",
    "1. Frobenius Norm Error (from the Eckart–Young theorem)\n",
    "\n",
    "The Eckart–Young theorem states that the rank-k SVD approximation $A_k$ is the best possible rank-k approximation of A in the Frobenius norm sense. Moreover, the error has an exact closed form that requires no reconstruction at all:\n",
    " \n",
    "$$\\|A - A_k\\|_F = \\sqrt{\\sigma_{k+1}^2 + \\sigma_{k+2}^2 + \\cdots + \\sigma_r^2}$$\n",
    " \n",
    "We simply sum the squares of the discarded singular values. This is computationally free once the SVD is computed, and it gives us a theoretically grounded lower bound - no other rank-k matrix can do better.\n",
    " "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 104,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def frobenius_error_exact(image, k):\n",
    "    total_sq = 0.0\n",
    "    for c in range(3):\n",
    "        _, s, _ = np.linalg.svd(image[:, :, c], full_matrices=False)\n",
    "        total_sq += np.sum(s[k:] ** 2)\n",
    "    return np.sqrt(total_sq)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "2. Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio\n",
    " \n",
    "PSNR is metric for lossy compression, expressed in decibels:\n",
    " \n",
    "$$\\text{PSNR} = 20 \\log_{10}\\!\\left(\\frac{255}{\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}}}\\right)$$\n",
    " \n",
    "A higher value means the reconstructed image is closer to the original. Values above ~30 dB are generally considered good quality; above 40 dB the difference is imperceptible to the human eye. We use PSNR to compare SVD and DCT on a common scale."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 105,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def psnr(original, reconstructed):\n",
    "    mse = np.mean((original - reconstructed) ** 2)\n",
    "    if mse == 0:\n",
    "        return float('inf')\n",
    "    return 20 * np.log10(255.0 / np.sqrt(mse))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "3. Structural Similarity Index\n",
    " \n",
    "SSIM measures perceptual similarity by comparing contrast and local structure between two images, rather than raw pixel differences. It ranges from 0 (no similarity) to 1 (identical). SSIM correlates much better with human visual perception than MSE or PSNR - two images can have the same PSNR but look very different if the errors occur at edges rather than in flat regions.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 106,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "def ssim_score(original, reconstructed):\n",
    "    orig_u8 = np.clip(original, 0, 255).astype(np.uint8)\n",
    "    rec_u8  = np.clip(reconstructed, 0, 255).astype(np.uint8)\n",
    "    scores = [ssim(orig_u8[:, :, c], rec_u8[:, :, c]) for c in range(3)]\n",
    "    return np.mean(scores)\n",
    " "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We use all three metrics together: Frobenius error gives the theoretically optimal LA bound, PSNR gives a standardized numerical score, and SSIM tells us how the result actually looks to a human viewer."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 107,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def explained_variance(image):\n",
    "    singular_values = []\n",
    "    for c in range(3):\n",
    "        s = np.linalg.svd(image[:, :, c], compute_uv=False, full_matrices=False)\n",
    "        singular_values.append(s)\n",
    "\n",
    "    singular_values = np.array(singular_values)\n",
    "    sv_mean = np.mean(singular_values, axis=0)\n",
    "    energy_by_rank = np.sum(singular_values ** 2, axis=0)\n",
    "    cumulative = np.cumsum(energy_by_rank) / np.sum(energy_by_rank)\n",
    "    return sv_mean, cumulative"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "DCT compression:\n",
    "The Discrete Cosine Transform is the mathematical foundation of the JPEG standard and the most widely deployed image compression technique in practice. Its core idea is a change of basis: instead of representing the image in the pixel domain, we transform it into the frequency domain using cosine functions as basis vectors.\n",
    " \n",
    "The 2-D DCT of an image block P is computed as:\n",
    " \n",
    "$$D = C \\cdot P \\cdot C^T$$\n",
    " \n",
    "where C is the DCT matrix - an orthogonal matrix. This means the transform is a pure rotation in the vector space of image patches; no information is lost at this stage.\n",
    " \n",
    "The compression comes next. Natural images have most of their energy concentrated in low-frequency cosine components (smooth regions, gradual gradients), while high-frequency components (sharp edges, fine textures) carry little energy and are harder for the human eye to perceive. As described in (https://medium.com/data-science/image-compression-dct-method-f2bb79419587), the standard JPEG pipeline divides the image into 8×8 blocks, applies DCT to each block, and then quantizes the result - zeroing out or high-frequency coefficients. In our simplified implementation we apply DCT to the full image and keep only the fraction of coefficients with the largest magnitudes.\n",
    " \n",
    "Applying DCT is a projection of the image onto a fixed orthonormal cosine basis C. Discarding small coefficients in this basis is the DCT analogue of rank-k truncation in SVD. The critical difference is that the cosine basis is fixed - it is the same for every image regardless of content."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def dct_compress(channel, keep_fraction):\n",
    "    keep_fraction = float(np.clip(keep_fraction, 0.0, 1.0))\n",
    "    coeffs = dctn(channel, norm='ortho')\n",
    "    if keep_fraction == 0:\n",
    "        return np.zeros_like(channel)\n",
    "    if keep_fraction == 1:\n",
    "        return idctn(coeffs, norm='ortho')\n",
    "    threshold = np.percentile(np.abs(coeffs), (1 - keep_fraction) * 100)\n",
    "    compressed = np.where(np.abs(coeffs) >= threshold, coeffs, 0)\n",
    "    return idctn(compressed, norm='ortho')\n",
    " \n",
    " \n",
    "def dct_compress_image(image, keep_fraction):\n",
    "    approx = np.zeros_like(image, dtype=np.float32)\n",
    "    for c in range(3):\n",
    "        approx[:, :, c] = dct_compress(image[:, :, c], keep_fraction)\n",
    "    return np.clip(approx, 0, 255)\n",
    " \n",
    " \n",
    "def dct_nonzero_to_svd_k(image, keep_fraction):\n",
    "    m, n = image.shape[:2]\n",
    "    total_coeffs = m * n\n",
    "    kept = int(keep_fraction * total_coeffs)\n",
    "    k = max(1, int(kept / (m + n + 1)))\n",
    "    return k"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "all metrics across a range of k values"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "max_k_for_comparison = min(min(m, n), compression_limit)\n",
    "k_values = list(range(1, max_k_for_comparison + 1, max(1, max_k_for_comparison // 40)))\n",
    "sv_mean, cumvar = explained_variance(image)\n",
    " \n",
    "frob_errors, psnr_svd, ssim_svd = [], [], []\n",
    "psnr_dct, ssim_dct = [], []\n",
    "storage_ratios = []\n",
    " \n",
    "for k in k_values:\n",
    "    rec_svd = reconstruct_image(image, k)\n",
    "    frob_errors.append(frobenius_error_exact(image, k))\n",
    "    psnr_svd.append(psnr(image, rec_svd))\n",
    "    ssim_svd.append(ssim_score(image, rec_svd))\n",
    " \n",
    "    ratio = compressed_size(k, image) / original_size\n",
    "    storage_ratios.append(ratio)\n",
    "    rec_dct = dct_compress_image(image, keep_fraction=ratio)\n",
    "    psnr_dct.append(psnr(image, rec_dct))\n",
    "    ssim_dct.append(ssim_score(image, rec_dct))\n",
    " \n",
    "print(\"Metrics computed for\", len(k_values), \"rank values.\")"
   ]
  },
